window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"18-10","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"18-10-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-01/","title_he":"א – הטהרה במים","title_en":"Purification in Water","body_en":"<p>It is a Torah mitzva that all matters of sanctity in the Temple service, as well as touching the meat of the sacrifices, ḥalla, and the produce of teruma and ma'aser sheni, and eating them, be carried out by people who are tahor (ritually pure). As we learned (above 1:2), tum'a (ritual impurity) is connected to death; therefore the most severe tum'a is that of a corpse, which is called 'avi avot ha-tum'a' (the ultimate source of impurity). A less severe tum'a is the tum'a caused by discharges that could have brought life and were lost, such as the tum'a of nidda (a menstruant woman) and of semen, or by discharges that reflect damage to the life-bringing system, such as the tum'a of a zav and a zava. The order of the purification process corresponds to the severity of the tum'a: one who became tamei through a corpse must wait seven days; on the third and the seventh day, water containing the ashes of the red heifer must be sprinkled upon him, and on the seventh day he immerses, and at its end he becomes tahor. A zav and a zava must wait seven clean days (shiv'a neki'im) and immerse, without any need for sprinkling of the red heifer's ash water. A man or a woman who became tamei through semen, as well as those who touch the carcass of a domestic or wild animal or a creeping creature (sheretz), are tamei until the end of the day and become tahor through immersion. The laws of purity are very numerous, and an entire order out of the six orders of the Mishna is devoted to them; however, one rule applies to all who are tamei: their purification is not complete without tevila (immersion) in water.</p>\n<p>As for the reason for the mitzva to become purified in water, one may say that water preceded the appearance of life, as it is stated (Bereishit 1:1-2): \"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth. And the earth was unformed and void, and darkness was upon the face of the deep, and the spirit of God hovered over the face of the <b>water</b>.\" On the first day God created light, in order to give direction to the world through it, and the world was still full of water and surrounded by it. On the second day (ibid. 6-7): \"And God said: Let there be a firmament in the midst of the water, and let it divide between water and water. And God made the firmament, and divided between the water which was under the firmament and the water which was above the firmament, and it was so.\" And the entire earth was still flooded with water. On the third day: \"And God said: Let the water under the heavens be gathered together to one place, and let the dry land appear, and it was so. And God called the dry land earth, and the gathering of the water He called seas, and God saw that it was good\" (ibid. 9-10). Thus water is the foundation from which life in the world began, and one who is tamei, who has become distanced from the root of life, goes back and immerses entirely in water, and thereby returns to the root of his vitality, and is as if created anew, and becomes tahor (more on this above 1:11-12).</p>\n<p>After the Temple was destroyed and Israel was exiled from its land, purity ceased from Israel; yet an important and sanctified purity still remained for us, namely purification from the tum'a of nidda, because the tum'a of nidda concerns not only entering the Temple and eating sacred foods, but also the closeness between husband and wife. Indeed, in the sanctified bond between a man and his wife the essence of purity and holiness in Israel has remained, as Rabbi Akiva said of a man and a woman who merit to live in faithfulness, that the Shekhina dwells between them (Sota 17a), for the root of the love, life and unity between a man and his wife is in the Holy of Holies (above 1:9-10; Peninei Halakha: Simḥat Ha-bayit U-virkhato 1:5-7).</p>\n<p>Because of the great stature of the holiness of Israel, and because the mikva (ritual bath) is used regularly by a large public of women, the custom of Israel is to be meticulous lekhatḥila (from the outset) in the laws of the mikva, and when possible, they are stringent to build it in a way that will be kosher even according to individual opinions that were not accepted as halakha (Tashbetz 1:17; Da'at Kohen 116). However, when the stringency would make it difficult to establish a mikva, one should not be stringent. All the more so, one should not be stringent with new stringencies, which would cast aspersions on the women who immersed in the past (Maharik 56; Da'at Kohen 117; 125).</p>\n<p>Because of the great importance of the mikva, every community is obligated to build a mikva (Rema, ḤM 163:3). Likewise, it is a mitzva to build a mikva that is as beautiful, clean and pleasant as possible (Ḥiddushei Rabbi Akiva Eiger 39).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"18-10-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-02/","title_he":"ב – מים טהורים בקרקע","title_en":"Pure Water in the Ground","body_en":"<p>A person's purification from his tum'a is accomplished by immersing the entire body at once in pure water that is gathered in the ground, that is, in groundwater that issued from a spring, or in rainwater that collected in the ground. For as long as this water is in the ground in its natural way, it is pure and cannot become tamei, as it is stated (Vayikra 11:36): \"Only a spring or a cistern, a gathering of water, shall be pure.\" But water that was detached from the ground by a person or by vessels can become tamei, and consequently, when someone who is tamei touches it, the water becomes tamei and can no longer purify him (Ḥatam Sofer, YD 213).</p>\n<p>Any place in the ground in which water can gather is fit for a mikva, including a spring, a sea, and a pit hewn in the ground. A pool built of bricks and concrete inside a building or on its roof, even atop a hundred stories, is also fit for a mikva. For since the building is attached to the ground in the manner of a building, the pool built above it is also considered attached to the ground. But water collected inside a vessel, even if the vessel is huge, such as a plastic pool or a ship, can become tamei, since it is not gathered in a place that is attached to the ground, and it is consequently unfit for a mikva.</p>\n<p>One who wishes to make a mikva by means of a large vessel, such as a plastic or metal tank, must first nullify its status as a vessel by piercing a hole in its bottom with a diameter of about eight cm. In addition, he must attach the pierced vessel to the ground with concrete or the like, or by placing it in a deep excavation in the ground. After the pierced vessel is attached to the ground, the hole is sealed by the ground, and one may additionally seal it with any material (Rambam; SA 201:7).</p>\n<p>When building a mikva, one may use iron rods to reinforce the concrete, even though under certain conditions they can become tamei, because anything that is attached to the ground and is not a vessel is nullified to the ground.<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>In addition to the requirement that the water of the mikva be gathered in the ground, it must meet three rules (which will be explained in halakhot 7-8): a) that it not be drawn (she'uvim) in vessels; b) that its formation (its creation) not be by a person, but by the forces of nature; c) that it not come to be by means of something that is 'susceptible to tum'a'.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The hole that nullifies the vessel must be in its bottom. Regarding its diameter, there are differing opinions: a) According to the Ra'ah and Ritva, the measure is 'kemotzi rimon' (large enough for a pomegranate to pass through). b) According to Rema 201:7, based on the Rosh, a hole 'kishfoferet ha-nod' (like the spout of a waterskin), which is a hole in which two fingers, the index finger and the middle finger, can be turned easily, whose measure is about 4 cm, and lekhatḥila 5 cm. c) According to the Gra 201:30, and Gidulei Tahara, Naḥal 16, Simla 25, it is the measure of 'a hole that purifies the vessel', that is, if the vessel had become tamei, the size of hole that nullifies its fitness for use purifies it. For metal and wooden vessels, it is 'kemotzi rimon', which is about a tefaḥ (7.6 cm), that is, a hole in which an ordinary fist can be turned (Mishna Kelim 17:1; Da'at Kohen 126). For earthenware vessels, it is 'kemotzi zayit' (large enough for an olive to pass through) (Kelim 3:1; about 2 cm). d) According to SA 201:7, based on the Rambam, it is the smaller of the measures of 'kishfoferet ha-nod' (4-5 cm) and of the hole that purifies the vessel (2 cm for earthenware vessels).<br>According to the Rash and the Rosh, in order to render the vessel fit to be a mikva it suffices to make a hole in it and to place it on the ground (and by placing it on the ground the hole will be sealed). According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Mikva'ot 6:4), it does not suffice to place the vessel on the ground; rather, it must also be attached to the ground, and so writes SA 201:7.<br>Bava Batra 65b: \"The Rabbis taught: A pipe that one hollowed out and afterwards fixed in place invalidates the mikva; if one fixed it in place and afterwards hollowed it out, it does not invalidate the mikva.\" According to most poskim, the law is the same regarding immersion in a vessel: if one took a piece of wood and attached it to the ground, and afterwards hollowed a vessel out of it, it is fit for tevila without any need to pierce it (Tur; Rema 7; Shakh 21; Pitḥei Teshuva 7; and others). Some are stringent, holding that only with regard to rabbinic laws is one lenient with 'fixed in place and afterwards hollowed out', but with regard to Torah laws one should be stringent and consider it a vessel, and therefore one should not immerse in it without making a hole in it (Gra 34; Responsa Rabbi Akiva Eiger 39). The Aḥaronim wrote that it is proper lekhatḥila to take their opinion into account (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 65; Da'at Kohen 127).<br>The Noda Bi-Yehuda (Tinyana, YD 142) put forward the novel view that a hole prepared in the bottom of a vessel in order to be sealed by fixing a faucet in it, through which water can be let out of the vessel, is not considered a hole that nullifies its status as a vessel. The Responsa Beit Efrayim (53) disagreed with him, but in practice was wary of being lenient without an additional hole besides the faucet. The Aḥaronim likewise take the opinion of the Noda Bi-Yehuda into account, as cited in Arukh Ha-shulḥan 77, and elsewhere. The poskim compared a faucet hole to the drain hole at the bottom of a sink or bathtub, which is not considered a hole that nullifies the vessel; therefore a bathtub is considered a vessel and is unfit for a mikva. However, in pressing circumstances, some permitted considering the drain hole as a hole that nullifies the bathtub's status as a vessel (Tevu'ot Shemesh, YD 63. And see below, note 13, that some hold that any vessel that is used only while attached to the ground is not considered a vessel)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-03/","title_he":"ג – שיעור מי המקווה – ארבעים סאה","title_en":"The Volume of a Mikva: Forty Se'a","body_en":"<p>The Sages learned from the verses of the Torah that the water of the mikva must be of a quantity in which a person can immerse his entire body at once, and not limb after limb. For this it must be of the measure of one amma by one amma, to a height of three ammot, since the height of an average person, without his head, is about three ammot, and when he immerses, the water rises up and the one immersing bends slightly, and thus his head enters the water. The volume of this measure is forty se'a (Ḥagiga 11a; Tosafot Yoma 31a, s.v. 'amma'). Even a person whose body is small, who is able to immerse his body in a smaller measure than this, cannot become tahor in a mikva that does not contain forty se'a, because only a mikva that is fit to purify people in general is kosher. Even if the mikva lacks any amount at all from forty se'a, it is invalid (Ketubot 104a). Therefore, if the mikva contained exactly forty se'a, and a person immersed in it and became tahor, then since he came up from the tevila with the drops on him, the mikva no longer contains forty se'a, and it becomes invalid (SA 201:62).</p>\n<p>In recent generations, doubt has arisen regarding the measures of the Sages, and consequently regarding the measure of forty se'a. According to the main opinion, the measure of a mikva is about 310 liters; some say at least 455 liters (Rav Ḥayim Na'eh), and some say about 650 liters (Ḥazon Ish). In practice, however, in order to avoid any doubt, and so that it will be comfortable to immerse, the custom is to prepare, lekhatḥila, mikva'ot that hold at least a thousand liters (a cubic meter). Even when one is forced to prepare a mikva of limited size, one stays as far as possible from the lowest measure, in order to keep away from the doubt.</p>\n<p>Although the measure of the mikva was set according to the measure of an amma by an amma to a height of three ammot, there is no requirement that this be its shape. This is the rule: any mikva that contains forty se'a of water, in which the one immersing can actually immerse his entire body at once, is kosher. However, lekhatḥila one should build the mikva in a shape that is comfortable for tevila (SA 201:1; 61; Rema 201:66).</p>\n<p>Only water is fit for a mikva, but other liquids, such as wine, oil and milk, are unfit for a mikva. Even if the mikva lacks a tiny amount (kortov) of water, it cannot be completed with wine, oil or milk (SA 201:24).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The water of the mikva must be of a measure into which the entire body of the one immersing can enter at once, as it is stated (Vayikra 15:16): \"And he shall bathe all his flesh in water,\" from which the Sages learned (Ḥagiga 11a): \"Water in which his entire body comes up. And how much is it? An amma by an amma, to a height of three ammot. And the Sages estimated: the water of a mikva is forty se'a.\" It is likewise stated regarding a person who became tamei (Vayikra 22:6-7): \"He shall not eat of the holy things unless he has bathed his flesh in water. And when the sun sets he shall be tahor…\" The Sages expounded in the Sifra: \"Could it be that he may wash limb by limb? The verse teaches: 'And when the sun sets he shall be tahor' – just as the setting of his sun is all at once, so too in the water, all of him at once.\" That is, just as the sun sets and disappears entirely, so too the tevila of the body must be of all of it at once. According to nearly all the Rishonim and Aḥaronim, the measure of forty se'a has Torah-level force (see Rivash 294, and Midot Ve-shi'urei Torah 17:27, and the notes there). According to the Levush 201:1 and the Baḥ 120:2, by Torah law the entire body must enter the water at once, and it was the Sages who set the measure of forty se'a.<br>The Taz (6) wrote that the measure of forty se'a suffices even for a very large person. However, the prevalent opinion is that it is a measure that suffices comfortably for an average person (Meiri, Nidda 67b; Rashba; Rivash; Tashbetz 34; Turei Even; Ḥelkat Binyamin 201, Tziyunim 45; and others). One may say that it is a measure that suffices for people in general: for ordinary people comfortably, and for very large people with difficulty.<br>A se'a is six kav; a kav is four log; a log is four revi'iyot (a revi'it is an egg and a half). According to the accepted calculation: a revi'it is 75 ml (Peninei Halakha: Berakhot 10, note 11), a log is 300 ml, a kav is 1.2 liters, a se'a is 7.2 liters, and forty se'a is 288 liters. These calculations of volume and length follow the ordinary amma, that is, the 'amma atzeva' (the strict amma), which is about 46 cm. However, regarding a mikva, as with other Torah laws, one must calculate according to the 'amma soḥeket' (the generous amma) (Eruvin 3b), that is, one adds another half-finger to it (Rashba), and the measure of half a finger is about 10 millimeters. Consequently, the measure of the mikva according to the accepted measure comes to about 310 liters. Rav Ḥayim Na'eh, being very stringent regarding various concerns and in calculating the amma soḥeket, wrote that it should not be less than 455 liters, and lekhatḥila 922 (Shi'ur Mikveh, section 74). According to the measure of the Ḥazon Ish, by calculation of the amma soḥeket it is 648, and lekhatḥila 750 (Shi'urin shel Torah, p. 64). In practice, the prevalent custom is to make mikva'ot of a thousand liters. However, be-di'avad (after the fact), one may rely on the accepted measure, which by calculation of the amma soḥeket is about 310 liters. (In Midot Ve-shi'urei Torah 4:8; 13:4, it is demonstrated that the measure of a revi'it according to the Rambam's measurement is about 74-76 ml. Regarding the laws of blessings, I adopted the average and convenient measure, that a revi'it is about 75 ml and an egg about 50 ml, since the Torah did not obligate a person to be exact in his eating and drinking by scientific measurement. However, regarding a mikva, which is a severe Torah law, and where the difference between the various measures amounts to several liters, we calculated according to the stringent calculation, by which a revi'it is 76 ml and forty se'a is about 292 liters, and with the addition of the amma soḥeket, about 310 liters.)<br>Lekhatḥila, the mikva is built so that the height of the water reaches about 125 or 120 cm, which is the comfortable height for tevila while bending slightly, which allows the water to reach every place on the body in the best way (Rema 201:66; above 5:5)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-04/","title_he":"ד – בין מעיין למקווה","title_en":"Between a Spring and a Mikva","body_en":"<p>There are two types of gatherings of water in the ground: a) a spring, whose source is groundwater, that is, water that issues from the ground; b) a pit in which rainwater collects, which is called a 'mikva'. As it is stated (Vayikra 11:36): \"Only a spring or a cistern, a gathering of water, shall be pure.\" Spring water is fit for tevila even while it is flowing, but the water of a mikva is fit only in an 'ashboren', that is, when it stands in its place, because water is fit in the manner in which it exists in its natural state. Rainwater comes down from the sky, and as long as it is flowing its state is temporary and not fixed; only when it collects in one place does it endure, and then it can be used. In contrast, a spring always issues forth and flows, and since this is its nature and the manner of its use, it is fit for tevila even while its water is flowing (Mishna Mikva'ot 1:7-8; SA 201:2).</p>\n<p>If another liquid, such as red wine, fell into a spring, then even though it changed the appearance of the water, since spring water is considered 'living water' (mayim ḥayim), the spring is fit for tevila. But if red wine fell into a mikva and changed the appearance of the water, then since the color of the mikva changed because of a liquid that is not water, the mikva becomes invalid. However, since the mikva already contained forty se'a of kosher water, one may add more drawn water to it until the mikva's appearance returns to the appearance of water, and it will be kosher. If the appearance of the mikva changed because the water became stale, or because vessels were rinsed in it, the mikva remains kosher (SA 201:25-28).</p>\n<p>A spring, like a mikva, requires a measure of forty se'a for the tevila of a person; but if the spring's water is flowing, all of the spring's water, from the place where it issues until the end of its flow, is combined for the calculation of forty se'a (SA 201:1).<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"<b>The measure of a spring for purifying a person</b>: The Sages said that a mikva purifies with forty se'a, and a spring with any amount (Mishna Mikva'ot 1:7; Torat Kohanim, Parashat Shemini). Some say that indeed a spring need not contain forty se'a in order to purify a person; all that is needed is that the one immersing actually immerse his entire body in it at once (Rambam, Hilkhot Mikva'ot 9:5-6; Ra'avad in Ba'alei Ha-nefesh). Others say that the statement that the measure of a spring is any amount refers only to the purification of vessels, but a person always needs forty se'a, which is the measure suited to the tevila of a person, as it is stated (Vayikra 15:16): \"all his flesh,\" and the Sages expounded (Ḥagiga 11a): \"Water in which his entire body comes up\" (Tosafot Nazir 38a, s.v. 'bar me-hahu'; Rash, Mikva'ot 5:2; Rabbeinu Tam; Ri. So too wrote the Rosh and Ritva, but it seems that in their view this law is rabbinic). The Rashba and the Ran ruled that one should follow the stringent opinion, since this involves a Torah-level doubt concerning a prohibition punishable by karet. And so it is ruled in SA 201:1.<br><b>Purification of vessels</b>: By Torah law, the measure for their purification is a mikva of a revi'it, and the Sages enacted that vessels too be immersed in a mikva of forty se'a (Nazir 38a). Regarding a spring, some say that one may indeed immerse thin vessels, such as needles, in a spring of any amount (Tosafot Nazir 38a, s.v. 'bar me-hahu'; Taz 201:1). Others say that at least a revi'it is needed (Mordekhai, cited in Darkhei Moshe 201:2). Regarding the tevila of vessels bought from a non-Jew, most poskim hold that forty se'a is needed, and so is the halakha (see Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 31:4, note 4).<br>One may explain that the power of a spring to purify with any amount is because it issues from the groundwater and is connected to it, and together with the groundwater it always contains more than forty se'a. So too wrote the Radbaz (Metzudat David 455), that a spring purifies with any amount and while flowing because its source is in water that has no end and does not cease. And Rav Yosef Karo wrote (Maggid Meisharim, Parashat Vayak'hel) that the spring comes from a supernal source, of a boundless heritage, and the sitra aḥra (the side of impurity) has no hold on it, and therefore it purifies even while flowing.<br>All those in the Torah who are tamei can become tahor through tevila in a mikva or in a spring, except for a zav, who can become tahor only in a spring, as it is stated (Vayikra 15:13): \"And when the zav is cleansed of his discharge, he shall count for himself seven days for his purification, and wash his clothes, and bathe his flesh in <b>living water</b>, and be tahor\" (Mishna Mikva'ot 1:8).<br>Three things specifically require living water, that is, spring water: a) the tevila of zavim; b) water for sprinkling on a metzora and on a house afflicted with tzara'at; c) water for the ashes of the red heifer."}]},{"slug":"18-10-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-05/","title_he":"ה – ים ונהר","title_en":"Sea and River","body_en":"<p>All seas, large and small, are fit for the tevila of people and of vessels. And although the water of the sea moves and sways because of the wind that creates waves, or flows because of rivers that enter it, the sea does not become invalid thereby; rather, just as a spring is fit even when its water flows by its nature, so too the sea is fit when its water flows or sways by its nature (Rashi and Tosafot, Shabbat 109a).</p>\n<p>A river whose source is an issuing spring has the law of a spring, and it is fit for tevila even while its water is moving and flowing. In contrast, a seasonal stream (naḥal akhzav), that is, a stream that flows as a result of the rains and afterwards stops flowing, has the law of a mikva, and it is fit only when its water is collected and standing (ashboren). A river whose source is a spring, but whose water increases during the rainy season and after the snows melt, follows the majority. When most of its water is from the spring, it is fit for tevila even though its water is flowing (zoḥalim). And when most of its water is from rain or from melted snow (notfim), it has the law of a mikva, and as long as it is flowing it is unfit for tevila. However, one may immerse in such a river by surrounding the place of tevila with a partition of matting, in such a way that the flow of the river is not noticeable within the partition, and then the water within the partition will be fit for tevila (SA 201:2).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"<b>Sea</b>: Mishna Mikva'ot 5:4: \"Rabbi Yosei says: All seas purify while flowing (like the law of a spring), and are unfit for zavim and for sanctifying the water of purification from them (like the law of a mikva).\" And so is the halakha (SA 201:5).<br><b>River</b>: Bekhorot 55b: according to Shmuel, any river whose source is a spring is fit for tevila, because most of its water is always spring water, for for every drop that reaches the river directly from the rain or from the melting snow, two drops come from the spring, which grows stronger as a result of the rains (based on Ta'anit 25b). So holds Rabbeinu Tam, the Rash, the Terumah and the Semag. In contrast, according to Rav and the father of Shmuel, when the river's water increases, it increases mainly from the rains or from the melting snow, and if most of the river's water is not from a spring, it is unfit for tevila while it is flowing. So holds Rabbeinu Ḥananel, the Rif, the Rambam and the Ramban. Only if the place of tevila in the river is surrounded by partitions of matting, in order to make the water stand as an ashboren, will it be fit for tevila. And so it is ruled in SA 201:2. Some say that in a place where there is no mikva, those who are lenient to immerse in a river whose majority is rain or snow water, following Rabbeinu Tam and those who agree with him, have a basis on which to rely (Terumat Ha-deshen 1:254; Maharik 115; Mahari Weil 70; Rema). Today, when it is easier to build mikva'ot or, alternatively, to travel to a place that has a kosher mikva, according to nearly all the Aḥaronim one should not immerse in a river except within a place surrounded by partitions, as I wrote above. (The Shakh 201:11 disagrees with the SA and the Rema and holds like the Maharik, that even when most of the river's water is from springs, if the river widened because of rainwater, one should not immerse in the place where it widened, but only in the place where the river would flow without the rainwater.)"}]},{"slug":"18-10-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-06/","title_he":"ו – מקווה נפסל בזחילה","title_en":"A Mikva Invalidated by Flowing","body_en":"<p>As we learned, a mikva is a gathering of water whose source is rainwater, and if its water is flowing (zoḥalim), it is disqualified from serving as a mikva. Therefore, one must take care that there be no leak from the mikva. Be-di'avad, if a crack opened in the mikva and water is flowing out of it: if, after all the water down to the height of the crack has flowed out, forty se'a will remain in the mikva, the mikva is kosher even while its water is flowing, since this flow will not cause its invalidation. But if, after all the water down to the height of the crack has flowed out, forty se'a will not remain in the mikva, then since, as a result of the flow, the mikva cannot endure, it is invalid, even though it still contains forty se'a. Only if the crack is sealed, and forty se'a remain in the mikva, will it become kosher again. When water is flowing out of a hole, one must seal it with something that is not susceptible to tum'a, such as sealing materials, or a plastic stopper, or a wooden one that has no receptacle, but not with a metal stopper or with a cloth (SA 201:50).<sup>5</sup></p>\n<p>Only a noticeable flow, which creates movement in the water, invalidates; but if the mikva's water flows out a little or is absorbed into the ground a little, as long as the flow is not noticeable and forty se'a remain in the mikva, the mikva is kosher (SA 201:51). Some are stringent even regarding a flow that is not noticeable (Gra). And although the main view follows the lenient opinion, lekhatḥila we take the stringent opinion into account. However, a slight drop in the water level, which is noticeable only after many hours, does not invalidate according to all opinions.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"According to the overwhelming majority of poskim, the disqualification of flowing is from the Torah (Terumat Ha-deshen 254; Maharik 115; Rema 201:2; Levush 2; Baḥ; Simla 7). Some say it is rabbinic (Mordekhai according to the Beit Yosef; Ḥatam Sofer, YD 202). And some say that a very noticeable flow invalidates by Torah law, and if it is not very noticeable, by the words of the Sages (Tzemaḥ Tzedek 165:5; Rashak).<br>When there is a hole in the mikva and below it there is room for forty se'a: according to the Rosh and SA 201:50, it is kosher. According to the Rash, it is invalid as long as the flow from the mikva continues, and according to the Rema, lekhatḥila one should take his view into account. The Me'il Tzedaka (39) and the Pitḥei Teshuva (3) wrote that since lekhatḥila we take the view of the Rash into account, the mikva should be built such that when the one immersing enters the water, water will not overflow out of the mikva without returning, even though forty se'a will remain in the mikva after the overflow. Some hold, based on what is explained in the Shakh 201:120, that even according to the Rash there is no need to be concerned about this, because as long as the overflow of water out of the mikva is caused by the one immersing, it is not an invalidating flow."},{"n":6,"en":"The Eshkol, the Rashba, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, the Meiri and the Tashbetz proved that a flow that is not noticeable does not invalidate, for if you do not say so, you would have no kosher mikva, since water seeps into the ground and is absorbed little by little. The Aḥaronim likewise inferred this from the opinion of the Rambam, who permitted immersing in a river by means of a mat that stops its flow, even though it has holes and gaps. And so too wrote SA 201:2. (However, Gidulei Tahara, Gaba 7, explains that this was permitted specifically in a river whose invalidity is rabbinic, since its basis is a spring, for by Torah law the majority of rainwater that mixed into it is nullified, because 'kamma kamma batel' (each bit is nullified as it enters).)<br>Some say that a noticeable flow is a flow that creates movement in the mikva's water (Sho'el U-meshiv Tinyana, part 4, 178; Maharsham 3:367; 370; Divrei Malkiel 3:64; Marḥeshet 1:39:1; Ḥazon Ish). It seems that in their view, as long as the flow does not create movement in the water, even if it is noticeable from the outside as a leak, it does not invalidate. And so too wrote the Maharsham 3:367 and Ein Yitzḥak 22. However, Da'at Kohen 103 was stringent when the leak is noticeable from the outside. Some are stringent even regarding a leak that is absorbed into the ground and is not noticeable from the outside, for the indication is that the mikva diminishes by the amount of a continuous leak, but a leak in the amount of dripping does not invalidate (Responsa Beit Shlomo 2:82; Imrei Yosher 1:127). And some were stringent even regarding a leak in the amount of continuous dripping (Divrei Ḥayim, YD 2:99).<br>The Gra (96) challenged the SA, which ruled that only a noticeable flow invalidates, for its words fit only the opinion of the Rash, that even if forty se'a will remain, the flow invalidates. But according to the Rosh, who holds that if forty se'a will remain in the mikva the flow does not invalidate, it follows that even when the flow is not noticeable, if because of it forty se'a will not remain in the mikva, it invalidates. And since the SA ruled like the Rosh, it should have been stringent in accordance with his view. However, many poskim disagree and hold that even according to the Rosh only a noticeable flow invalidates (Tashbetz 1:17; Tzemaḥ Tzedek, YD 164:1; Maharsham; and others). Some explain that even the Gra did not intend to be stringent (Sha'arei Tziyon 3:26). However, lekhatḥila we take his opinion into account according to the stringent understanding (Da'at Kohen 103; Mishneh Sakhir 139). Some wrote that even according to the Gra, only a crack or a hole invalidates, and not water seeping into the ground (Arugat Ha-bosem 212, and similarly in Dovev Meisharim 1:69; Ḥibbur Le-tahara 40). According to all opinions, if the mikva was checked and found to have no crack, there is no need to be concerned about a slight drop in the water level that is noticeable only after many hours (Shiyurei Berakha 201:4; Darkhei Teshuva 282; Divrei Malkiel 3:64). As was written in Ba'ei Ḥayei, section 198 (by the author of Knesset Ha-gedola): \"If the mikva was checked very thoroughly and no crack, small or large, was found in it at all, and the water within the ashboren is calm and tranquil, and does not stir or move to go out, but diminishes of its own accord, whether because it is absorbed into the ground, or whether because of the heat of the bathhouse it diminishes little by little, and its flow is not noticeable… it is fit to immerse in lekhatḥila according to all opinions, even though the water diminishes every day by about two or three finger-breadths.\""}]},{"slug":"18-10-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-07/","title_he":"ז – פסול שאובים","title_en":"The Disqualification of Drawn Water","body_en":"<p>Drawn water (mayim she'uvim), that is, water that was detached from the ground and was in vessels, is unfit for a mikva. Even when the water was not drawn by a person but reached the vessels from the sky, for example, if a person placed vessels in his courtyard in order to catch rainwater, the rainwater caught in the vessels he placed is considered drawn and is unfit for a mikva.</p>\n<p>Water that flows in pipes from springs and wells is not considered drawn; but if the pipe has a 'receptacle' (beit kibul), the pipe becomes a vessel, and the water that passes through it is considered drawn and is invalid. Since the water that the water company conveys through pipes often passes through receptacles, tap water reaching homes and gardens is considered drawn water (as will be explained below in halakha 12).</p>\n<p>Not only is drawn water unfit for a mikva, but if a quantity of three log (about 900 ml) of drawn water enters rainwater that does not amount to forty se'a, all the kosher rainwater becomes invalid. Even when the three log of drawn water fell into the rainwater unintentionally, all the kosher rainwater has become invalid. And even if another forty se'a of kosher water is added to it, the mikva remains invalid. The solution is to empty the mikva of all its water and to wait for it to fill again with rainwater. But if from the outset the mikva contained rainwater in the measure of forty se'a, then even if thousands of liters of drawn water are brought into it, it will not become invalid.</p>\n<p>Drawn water invalidates rainwater that does not amount to forty se'a provided that it fell directly into it; but if it was poured near the mikva and was drawn along the ground for a length of three tefaḥim (about 23 cm) and afterwards entered the kosher water, it was nullified to the ground and it too became kosher, provided that the rainwater constitutes the majority of the forty se'a. Therefore, if the mikva contained more than twenty se'a of rainwater, one may complete the mikva with drawn water that is poured near the mikva and is drawn along the ground into it (SA 201:44-45). This procedure is called 'hamshakha' (conveyance). The manner of hamshakha will be explained at the end of the next halakha.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"According to Rashi, the Rashbam, the Rosh and Rema 201:3, drawn water is invalid by Torah law; however, if only a minority of the water is drawn, the invalidity is by the words of the Sages. The Sages decreed that three log of drawn water invalidate rainwater as long as it does not contain the measure of a kosher mikva, because the measure of three log is a measure that barely suffices for a person to wash (Tosafot; Rosh). In contrast, according to the Rambam, the Ri and the Ramban, the invalidity of drawn water is entirely by the words of the Sages, lest people immerse in water that is inside vessels.<br><b>Hamshakha</b>: According to most Rishonim, only if the majority of the mikva's water (more than twenty se'a) is kosher water can drawn water be added to it through hamshakha (She'iltot; Razah; Ra'avad; Ramban; Rashba; Rambam 4:8; and SA 201:44). In contrast, according to the Rif as understood by the Ramban, Rashba and Ran, even if the entire mikva is of drawn water that was conveyed by hamshakha, it is kosher; and so wrote the Rambam (ibid. 4:9) in the name of the sages of the West. According to most poskim, even the stringent ones agree that the invalidity of drawn water conveyed by hamshakha is rabbinic (Tosafot Bava Batra 66b; Tashbetz 3:12; Maharit, YD 17; Ḥazon Ish Tinyana 4:14). And there is one who holds that according to the stringent ones the invalidity is from the Torah (Imrei Esh 86)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-08/","title_he":"ח – פסולי הווייתו על ידי אדם ובטומאה","title_en":"Invalidity of Water Formed by a Person or Through Tum'a","body_en":"<p>Even when the mikva was not filled by means of vessels that make the water drawn, for example, if a person filled it using a pierced vessel, or if there was a rain puddle beside the mikva and a person splashed water with his hands from the puddle into the mikva, the mikva is invalid, since its formation (its creation) was done by a person and not by Heaven, that is, by the forces of nature. Even when only three log of water reached, by means of a person, a mikva that did not yet contain forty se'a, all of it becomes invalid, like the law of drawn water that fell into a deficient mikva (SA 201:15; 39).</p>\n<p>A further condition for a pure mikva is that the water come into being in it in purity, as it is stated (Vayikra 11:36): \"Only a spring or a cistern, a gathering of water, shall be pure,\" and the Sages expounded (Zevaḥim 25b): \"Their formation shall be through purity.\" But if the rainwater entered the mikva by means of things that can become tamei, the water is invalid. Therefore, water that flowed into the mikva through a metal pipe that is not attached to the ground is invalid, since a metal pipe that is not attached to the ground is susceptible to tum'a. All this applies only when the water reached the mikva directly through something that is susceptible to tum'a; but if, after leaving the thing that is susceptible to tum'a, it continued to flow on the ground and then poured into the mikva, it is kosher, since in practice it entered the mikva without the aid of something that is susceptible to tum'a (SA 201:48).<sup>8</sup></p>\n<p>It is customary to end the conveyance of the rainwater that comes down from the roof into the mikva with a channel three tefaḥim long (about 23 cm), whose bottom is of soft concrete that can absorb water; thereby, even if an invalidity of 'drawn water', 'formed by a person' or 'formed through tum'a' befell the water, the invalidity will be remedied by this hamshakha.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"According to the Rambam, the Eshkol and the Yere'im, the invalidity of 'formed through tum'a' does not invalidate an ordinary mikva, but only disqualifies water conveyed from a spring from being considered a spring. However, according to the other Rishonim it invalidates any mikva, and so is the halakha, as explained above (SA 201:48).<br>According to the Rosh, the creation of a kosher mikva must be in purity, but once there is a kosher mikva, one may convey water from it through something that is susceptible to tum'a, such as a metal pipe, since the water is purified by its contact (hashaka) with the kosher mikva or with the spring. According to the Rashba, this too is forbidden. SA 201:49 ruled leniently without attribution, like the Rosh, and lekhatḥila the pipe should be left open in order to maintain the connection (Leḥem 201:190). Some were stringent like the Rashba (Baḥ; Da'at Kohen 127; and others).<br>According to the Rash (Mikva'ot 5:5), it is forbidden to seal a mikva whose water is flowing out with something that is susceptible to tum'a, such as a vessel or a cloth, for thereby its formation is by means of something that is susceptible to tum'a. According to the Rosh, the prohibition applies only to bringing the rainwater into the mikva, but preventing it from flowing out is permitted even with something that is susceptible to tum'a. SA 201:50 was stringent like the Rash in its unattributed ruling; therefore, when the mikva's drain hole is in its floor, it should be sealed with a stopper made of wood or plastic, and not of metal, which is susceptible to tum'a (Da'at Kohen 242). To avoid the problem, many are meticulous not to place the drain hole at the bottom of the mikva, but rather to drain its water by pumping.<br>As we learned above, the law of the invalidity of 'formed by a person' is like the law of the invalidity of drawn water, that even three log invalidate a mikva that does not yet contain forty se'a. Some say that the same applies to 'formed through tum'a', that three log invalidate a mikva (Mei Ha-shilo'aḥ 5:1; Simla 83). Others say that it invalidates only when it is the majority (Gidulei Tahara, Gaba 43; Avnei Nezer 272; Da'at Kohen 127, p. 236).<br>There are three types of materials with respect to the law of susceptibility to tum'a: a) Metal, which is susceptible to tum'a even if it has no receptacle. Therefore, a metal pipe is susceptible to tum'a even when it has no receptacle. However, if the metal pipe was made in order to be fixed in the ground or in the wall of a building, and it was actually fixed in the ground or in the building, it is nullified to the ground, and the water may be conveyed through it to the mikva. b) Earthenware, wood, leather and glass: if a vessel that has a receptacle was made from them, it is susceptible to tum'a, and if it has no receptacle, such as a pipe, it is not susceptible to tum'a. c) Stone vessels, even when they have a receptacle, are not susceptible to tum'a. Regarding <b>plastic vessels</b>, such as a plastic pipe that has a receptacle: according to nearly all poskim, their law is like that of stone vessels, which are not susceptible to tum'a (Ḥazon Ish 126:7; Igrot Moshe, YD 3:53; Badei Ha-shulḥan 190, 107; Taharat Ha-bayit 1, 428, where he wrote that this is also the opinion of Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv; Be-ohala shel Torah 1:25. And see Shevet Ha-levi 4:115, that their law may possibly be like that of glass vessels). All the more so, a stopper of rubber or plastic is not susceptible to tum'a, for even if the stopper were of wood, since it has no receptacle it is not susceptible to tum'a."},{"n":9,"en":"According to the She'iltot, the Rambam and the Ra'avad, and so it is ruled in SA 201:46, the hamshakha of drawn water may also be over stone that does not absorb. According to the Yere'im and the Roke'aḥ, it must specifically be over earth that is fit to absorb, so that the water is nullified in it and becomes again like water that seeped out of the ground; and the Rema wrote that lekhatḥila it is good to be stringent. And this is the practice. However, in a situation of some difficulty, one should not be stringent (Da'at Kohen 124; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 153). Regarding concrete, the poskim disagree: some hold that its law is like earth and one may convey water over it lekhatḥila (Maharshag 1, YD 66), and some hold that since its absorption is minimal, its law is like stone (Emek She'ela, YD 51). Soft concrete is fit for hamshakha according to all opinions.<br>In practice, the custom is to be meticulous to convey all water that enters the mikva over three tefaḥim of soft concrete. For we learned (in note 7) that according to some of the Rishonim, even if all the water is drawn and arrived by hamshakha, the mikva is kosher. And even according to most of the Rishonim, who were stringent, it is invalid only by rabbinic law, so that in a case of dispute, thanks to the hamshakha there is an additional rabbinic consideration (for example, 'zeri'a' in the otzar (reservoir), which according to the Ra'avad is invalid by rabbinic law under the law of 'he added a se'a and removed a se'a', as will be explained below in halakha 9; through the hamshakha it becomes a double doubt (sfek sfeka), where both doubts are rabbinic).<br>The law of hamshakha over the ground to remedy water formed through tum'a is more lenient than the law of hamshakha for drawn water. First, hamshakha in the case of formation through tum'a can remedy all the water of the mikva, whereas with drawn water, according to most Rishonim, it remedies only a minority of the mikva. Second, with formation through tum'a, a hamshakha of three tefaḥim is not required, but any amount suffices, and even according to the stringent opinions it need not be over ground that can absorb (SA 201:48). This is because drawn water was invalidated because it was inside a vessel, and in order to render it fit it must be reconnected to the ground, whereas with formation through tum'a there is no invalidity in the water itself, and it suffices that it actually reaches the mikva in purity (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 201:163).<br>The invalidity of 'formed by a person': some say that its law is lenient, like the law of formation through tum'a (Ḥatam Sofer, YD 200; Naḥal Eshkol 53:3). Others say that its law is severe, like the law of the invalidity of drawn water (Taz 28; Ginat Veradim, YD 4:4; Da'at Kohen 127)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-09/","title_he":"ט – זריעה והשקה","title_en":"Zeri'a and Hashaka","body_en":"<p>Once there is a kosher mikva, one can additionally create further mikva'ot by means of it, through 'zeri'a' (seeding) or through 'hashaka' (contact), as we shall explain:</p>\n<p><b>Zeri'a</b>: Just as a kosher mikva purifies those who immerse in it, so it purifies and renders fit drawn water that was 'sown' into it, that is, that fell or flowed into it. Even if a quantity of drawn water a thousand times greater is poured into a kosher mikva, all the water poured into it will turn into water fit for tevila. And if a great deal of water is sown into the mikva so that it overflows and fills many more pools, all the pools will be fit for tevila, since the water in them was rendered fit by zeri'a in a kosher mikva. Admittedly, there is one who holds that if no more than twenty se'a of the original rainwater remain in the mikva, the mikva becomes invalid by the words of the Sages (Ra'avad); but according to the rest of the Rishonim, a mikva can render fit through zeri'a an unlimited amount of water, and there is no need for original rainwater to remain in it, since all the water sown into it became kosher like rainwater. And so is the halakha (SA 201:15; 24).<sup>10</sup></p>\n<p><b>Hashaka</b>: Drawn water that was put in contact with mikva water, that is, that touched the mikva water, becomes fit for tevila. That is, if there was a kosher mikva and beside it a pool of drawn water, and they are connected by a hole with a diameter of 5 cm ('kishfoferet ha-nod'), then the moment the pool's water touches the mikva water, it is rendered fit, and the pool becomes a kosher mikva. Even if the hole between the mikva and the pool is afterwards sealed, the water in the pool has already become a kosher mikva and will not become invalid again. Admittedly, there is one who holds that if the hole connecting them is sealed again, the water in the pool becomes unfit for tevila (Rabbeinu Yeruḥam). But according to halakha, once the pool's water has been in contact with the water of the otzar one time, the pool's water remains kosher forever (SA 201:52; and see Shakh 112. Nowadays, when the tevila pool is filled through hamshakha, even according to the stringent opinion the invalidity is rabbinic, as explained above in halakha 7 and note 7).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Mishna Mikva'ot 7:2: \"If it contained forty se'a (of water), and one added a se'a (of fruit juice) and removed a se'a (of water), it is kosher.\" In Yevamot 82b it is explained that 'added a se'a and removed a se'a' means \"up to its majority.\" According to most Rishonim (Rashi, Rabbeinu Tam, the Rash, the Rashba, the Rosh and others), the meaning is that if liquids that are not water, such as fruit juice, were put into a mikva that contains forty se'a of kosher water, and correspondingly water was removed from it, then when the liquids that are not water have become the majority of the forty se'a, the water is nullified and the mikva becomes invalid. But if drawn water fell into a kosher mikva, it never becomes invalid, since all water that joined the mikva became kosher in itself. In contrast, according to the Ra'avad (Ba'alei Ha-nefesh, Sha'ar Ha-mayim 1), this law was stated concerning drawn water: if a mikva contained forty se'a, and drawn water was poured into it and correspondingly kosher water went out of the mikva, then if no more than twenty se'a of original water that is not drawn remain in the mikva, which is the measure of the majority of a mikva, the mikva is invalid by rabbinic law (see Ḥelkat Binyamin 201, Tziyunim 1264). The Beit Yosef explained that the Rambam (4:6-7) agrees with the Ra'avad in a case where a person poured drawn water into the mikva and correspondingly took water out of the mikva with his hands: if no more than twenty se'a of original water that is not drawn remain, the mikva should be invalidated out of concern for appearances (mar'it ayin), lest people think that drawn water is fit for a mikva. But if one added drawn water to the 'otzar zeri'a' (seeding reservoir), and the water in the otzar overflowed by itself into the tevila pool, as is the practice in our mikva'ot, then even if no more than twenty se'a of original kosher water remain in the otzar zeri'a, it remains kosher. As halakha, SA 201:24 ruled according to most Rishonim. However, in the Beit Yosef he cited the words of the Tashbetz 1:17, that when possible, it is proper to take into account the opinion of the Ra'avad and the Rambam. And so too wrote the Shakh 63.<br>The law of a spring is like the law of a mikva: it purifies and renders fit drawn water that fell into it through zeri'a (SA 201:15; see Rema 201:40), and it purifies and renders fit drawn water that came into contact with it by way of hashaka, which is explained further on in the halakha (Mishna Mikva'ot 5:1)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-10/","title_he":"י – המקוואות בימינו","title_en":"Mikva'ot in Our Times","body_en":"<p>In the past, many were accustomed to immerse in springs, seas and rivers, for at a time when there was no system of pipes for supplying water, people needed to live near water sources, and consequently they used them for tevila. In places that were not near available water sources, it was customary to hew pits in the courtyard to store rainwater, and one of the pits was devoted to tevila. Following the development of solutions for transporting water and the growth of the urban population, the need arose to build mikva'ot that would serve many people. Then, if everyone immersed in the same mikva, the water would become dirty; therefore they separated the 'otzar' (reservoir) of rainwater from the tevila pool, which they would render fit by zeri'a in the otzar or by hashaka with it. In this way they could change the water in the tevila pool frequently.</p>\n<p>In recent generations, with the rise in the standard of living, many began to be meticulous to render the water in the tevila pool fit in both ways, through zeri'a and through hashaka. That is, the mikva is rendered fit by means of two 'otzarot', each of which contains forty se'a of rainwater: a) an 'otzar zeri'a' (seeding reservoir), into which tap water is conveyed, and from which the water overflows through hamshakha into the tevila pool; b) an 'otzar hashaka' (contact reservoir), with which the tevila pool is put in contact through a hole whose diameter is at least 5 cm. At the time of tevila the hole is opened, and when no one is immersing it is closed, so that the water in the 'otzar hashaka' will not be soiled by the water in which people immersed. However, even if the hole connecting the tevila pool to the otzar hashaka was closed at the time of tevila, the tevila is valid, since opening the hole is an enhancement (hiddur) (see Igrot Moshe, YD 1:112).<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The advantage of making two otzarot: we learned in the previous note that according to the Ra'avad, by the words of the Sages, an otzar zeri'a is not effective when in practice most of the water that went out of it is drawn; in that case the otzar hashaka, which contains more than twenty se'a of rainwater, solves the problem. On the other hand, we learned in halakha 9 that according to Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, an otzar hashaka is effective only while the hole between it and the tevila pool is open, and there is a concern that it will be closed in order to preserve the water of the otzar; in that case, if the water in the tevila pool is also rendered fit by the otzar zeri'a, then even when the hole between the pool and the otzar hashaka is closed, the mikva will be kosher even according to the approach of Rabbeinu Yeruḥam.<br>However, even when a mikva is made in both ways, according to certain opinions there is still a problem. For even if they are careful to open the stopper between the otzar and the tevila pool at the times of tevila, in order to take Rabbeinu Yeruḥam's opinion into account, and also careful to seal it outside the times of tevila, in order to minimize the passage of water from the otzar to the tevila pool and thereby prevent the invalidation of the otzar according to the Ra'avad, there is still a concern that over time no more than 20 se'a of rainwater will remain in the otzar hashaka, and then, according to the Ra'avad, the otzar becomes invalid. And if they make an effort to keep the stopper between the tevila pool and the otzar hashaka always closed, there is a concern that sometimes they will forget to open it at the times of tevila, and then, according to Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, the tevila will not be valid. Because of these concerns, some are accustomed to build the otzar hashaka beneath the mikva (Chabad), and since the custom is to be meticulous to heat the water in the tevila pool, it does not mix with the colder water of the otzar hashaka.<br>However, there is no need to be concerned for this stringency, for the opinions of the Ra'avad and Rabbeinu Yeruḥam are individual opinions that were not accepted as halakha. In addition, it seems that even according to the Ra'avad, the otzar becomes invalid only when the outflow of the rainwater is noticeable. But if the water in the 'otzar hashaka' is exchanged with the water in the pool so slowly that it is not noticeable, then even if no more than twenty se'a of original rainwater remain there, the otzar is kosher, as he learned from the Mishna (Mikva'ot 7:2) and the Gemara (Yevamot 82b): \"He added a se'a and removed a se'a – it is kosher… up to its majority,\" which implies that if the rainwater diminished in a way that is not noticeable, it is kosher even beyond its majority. This is also implied by the opinion of all the Aḥaronim who encouraged making an 'otzar hashaka' in addition to an 'otzar zeri'a' in order to satisfy the opinion of the Ra'avad. In addition, nowadays the custom is to convey the water entering the tevila pool through a channel three tefaḥim long of soft concrete, and thereby, even if all the water is drawn, some say that it is kosher even without hashaka and zeri'a; and even according to most poskim, who are stringent where there is no hashaka and zeri'a, it is invalid only by the words of the Sages (as explained in notes 7; 9). In addition, even according to the Ra'avad, the invalidity of 'added a se'a and removed a se'a' is rabbinic. Thus we are dealing with a concern for individual opinions, in a double doubt (sfek sfeka) in a rabbinic law. In addition, Igrot Moshe (YD 3:65) wrote that even according to this stringency, although the process of the water mixing is slower, in practice after several weeks no more than twenty se'a of original rainwater will remain in the lower 'otzar hashaka'. In any case, the custom of Israel has the force of law, and so in practice did the great Torah scholars of Israel act, relying on the opinion of the overwhelming majority of poskim, and their wives immersed in a mikva based on an otzar hashaka or zeri'a. And if we wish to be meticulous, the prevalent enhancement of using two otzarot, 'zeri'a' and 'hashaka', suffices for us, and no more. Moreover, someone has raised the argument that an otzar beneath the mikva is not considered hashaka but rather 'katafras' (a connection on a slope and not at an equal level), which is not a connection to the mikva (based on the words of Divrei Ḥayim 2:88), in which case a tevila pool made by the Chabad method would be unfit for a mikva. And although this is a far-fetched argument (see Taharat Ha-bayit, Mikva'ot 2), it is no more far-fetched than the argument of the stringent ones, who do not rely on nearly all the poskim in a rabbinic law.<br>The Mordekhai (Mikva'ot 750) wrote that one should not put a mikva of hot water in contact with a river, nor pour hot water into a mikva, because of the decree concerning bathhouses, lest people mistakenly think that a bathhouse whose water is drawn is also fit for tevila. According to the Hagahot Mordekhai, it is permitted to heat the mikva, and so holds the Ra'avya and the Riva. SA 201:75 mentioned the opinion of the Mordekhai, and the Rema wrote that one should be stringent, but one should not protest against those who are lenient in a place where that is the custom. In practice, the Aḥaronim wrote that one should not be stringent in this, since the stringency could cause some women not to immerse, or to immerse hastily, or to get cold and fall ill (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 216; Da'at Kohen 126; Sha'arei Mikva'ot p. 376; and many others). Our master Rav Kook further explained in Da'at Kohen 125 that when the entire mikva is heated, even the stringent ones would agree that it is permitted, since this is not the manner of using drawn water, for the decree concerning bathhouses applied where the water was heated in vessels and poured from them into the mikva. But when the entire tevila pool is heated, there is no place for the decree concerning bathhouses.<br>It is permitted, and even desirable, to install a filter inside the mikva in order to filter the water, but because of various concerns it should not be installed outside the water (Har Tzvi, YD 177; Taharat Ha-bayit, Mikva'ot 4)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-11/","title_he":"יא – מקווה מקרח ושלג שהופשרו","title_en":"A Mikva from Melted Ice and Snow","body_en":"<p>As we learned (in halakha 7), drawing invalidates water; but if the water solidified and became snow, hail or ice, it is not subject to the invalidity of drawing, for even if it is put into a vessel, since it can stand without it, it is not thereby considered drawn and does not become unfit for a mikva. Moreover, even drawn water that has already been disqualified from being used to make a mikva: if it freezes and turns into ice, the invalidity of drawn water is removed from it, and after it melts it becomes water fit for a mikva once again. Therefore, when there is a need to make a mikva in the summer, instead of waiting until the rains come to fill the otzar, one may fill it with water melted from ice.</p>\n<p>This is how it is done: water is frozen in a freezing room and turned into blocks of ice; the blocks of ice are transported on wooden platforms that are not vessels, and the blocks of ice are placed to melt near the mikva, so that the water coming out of the ice is drawn along the ground and enters the mikva. Admittedly, some have reservations about this out of concern for various individual opinions, and therefore lekhatḥila it is preferable to make a mikva with rainwater; but when there is a need, one may prepare a mikva by bringing blocks of ice (Da'at Kohen 94; 98).<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"In Mishna Mikva'ot 7:1 we learned that water coming from snow, hail and frost is fit for a mikva and does not invalidate it under the law of drawn water, and so it is ruled in the Rambam, Hilkhot Mikva'ot 7:3. Therefore, snow and ice that were brought in a vessel do not become drawn, and it is permitted to make a mikva from them. So too wrote the Rosh, the Tur and SA 201:30. The reason is that since they can stand without a vessel, putting them into a vessel does not make them drawn (Simla 62). However, according to the Razah, if they were carried in a vessel, they became drawn and are unfit for a mikva.<br>Even water that was drawn and became invalid, if it was frozen and became ice, loses the status of drawn water, and when it melts it becomes fit for a mikva. So wrote the Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, the Semag, the Darkhei Moshe, SA 201:31, and the Shakh 74. Their source is the Tosefta (Teharot 2:3): \"A mikva of drawn water that froze is pure with respect to drawn water; once it melted, it is fit to be gathered [as a mikva].\" Therefore, many poskim permitted, when there is a need, preparing a mikva from drawn water that was frozen and melted, among them: Da'at Kohen 94; 98; Rav Pe'alim, part 2, YD 24; Divrei Malkiel 3:68; Maharshag 1:62; Aḥi'ezer 3:33:2; 4:39; Mishpetei Uziel, YD, Mahadura Tinyana 34; Taharat Ha-bayit, part 3, pp. 307-323.<br>However, some poskim, because of various concerns, preferred to avoid making a mikva by bringing blocks of ice, and some of them even ruled to be stringent in this unless in absolute necessity. Some were concerned for the opinion of the Razah, that snow and ice carried in a vessel become invalid as drawn water (Ḥatam Sofer, YD 200; Har Tzvi, YD 179; Divrei Malkiel 3:68). To address this problem, the blocks of ice are transported on wooden platforms that are not vessels and are not susceptible to tum'a. Some are concerned that placing the ice in the mikva, or melting it with a heating element near the mikva, invalidates the water under the law of 'formed by a person' (Beit Shlomo 2:76; Imrei Yosher 1:148; Rav Pe'alim, part 2, YD 24). These concerns are resolved by natural melting of the ice near the mikva, in such a way that the water flows along the ground and so reaches the mikva (Da'at Kohen 97; see Divrei Malkiel 3:67-68; Aḥi'ezer 3:33).<br>Some are concerned for a first opinion in the Semag, according to which drawn water that froze remains invalid (Simla 63; Levush Mordekhai 25). And some are concerned that if the water was frozen artificially, by a person, the status of drawn water is not removed from it (U-metzur Devash, YD 11). These problems cannot be resolved; however, according to the rules of halakha, when there is a need there is no reason to be concerned for these individual opinions, especially since according to most poskim the invalidity of drawn water that was conveyed into the mikva by hamshakha is rabbinic (above, note 7).<br>Many hold that snow is fit while it is still snow, and so wrote Rabbeinu Shemaryah. However, Rabbeinu Simḥa wrote that it becomes fit only after it melts, because while it is still snow, the entire body of the one immersing does not come into full contact with the snow, due to the gaps that form between the snow and the body. The Rema 201:30 cited both opinions and concluded: \"And it is good to be stringent lekhatḥila.\" The Shakh (71) wrote that one should not immerse in snow and ice until they melt, and Da'at Kohen 89 wrote that this is the accepted ruling."}]},{"slug":"18-10-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-12/","title_he":"יב – דין המים הזורמים בצינורות לבתים","title_en":"The Law of Water Flowing in Pipes to Homes","body_en":"<p>The water flowing in the system of pipes that spans the whole country generally originates in water that is fit for a mikva. In the past it came from rainwater that collected in lakes, from groundwater and from springs. Later on they began to purify sewage water, and since at the end of the purification process the water is absorbed into the ground and joins the groundwater, it becomes kosher (SA 201:47). Today, most of the water conveyed in pipes in the State of Israel is desalinated seawater, and it too is in principle fit for a mikva, since it came from the sea.</p>\n<p>The question is whether a mikva can be made from water that flowed through the system of pipes. The law depends on the question of whether they contain a receptacle. For if the pipes have a receptacle, such as a place for collecting debris, the water flowing through them becomes invalid like drawn water (above, halakha 7; Mishna Mikva'ot 4:3; SA 201:36).</p>\n<p>In practice, the custom is to consider the water that the water company conveys in pipes to homes and gardens as drawn water, because it often passes through a receptacle, such as the receptacle made for housing the pump or the water meter, or for separating out debris and salts. All the more so when it passes through tanks on top of water towers or beside them, or when it passes through storage tanks or water heaters on the roofs. And although the pipes with the receptacle are attached to the ground, since they had a receptacle before they were attached to the ground, even after being attached to the ground they are still considered vessels, and consequently the water passing through them is considered drawn.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"There have been many discussions and doubts on this subject, and therefore the poskim ruled that one should not use water flowing in pipes for a mikva (Da'at Kohen 95; Minḥat Yitzḥak 5:82; Igrot Moshe, YD 3:63; Mavo Le-hilkhot Mikva'ot by Rav Yehoshua ben Meir, p. 48). However, there were poskim who examined the water system in their city and found that the water flowing in the pipes was kosher (Gidulei Tahara, Responsa 5; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 201:169; U-metzur Devash 4; Zivḥei Tzedek, YD 22; Taharat Mayim, section 40). In general, those inclined to leniency base themselves on the fact that according to many Rishonim the invalidity of drawn water is rabbinic, especially when, after the water leaves the faucet, it is conveyed over the ground (see above, notes 7, 9-10). Therefore, only when the pipes had a widening for receiving debris, or when the water passed through tanks intended for storing water, did they forbid the water; but in all other doubts they inclined to leniency. In contrast, the stringent ones followed the accepted line, that in matters of mikva one takes individual opinions into account lekhatḥila, and therefore they were stringent not to use tap water even in a situation where according to most poskim it is kosher. A further reason for their stringency is that it is difficult to check the entire system, which changes frequently, and there is concern that it contains an invalidity that is not known. Sometimes the invalidity is clear-cut, for example, when there are water storage tanks above the houses and the tap water passes through them, and then according to all opinions the water is drawn. Likewise, there are places where the water passes through a tank on top of a water tower or beside it, in order to increase the pressure of the water conveyed to homes, and then, as long as the tank is not considered a building, the water passing through it is considered drawn.<br>The sources of the water in the pipes are fit for a mikva, for they come from lakes, rivers and springs. Likewise, <b>desalinated seawater</b> is fit for a mikva, for in the first stage the water is stored in pools built in the ground, and afterwards it undergoes the desalination process by being forced at high pressure into pressure vessels containing super-filters (membranes) made mainly of glass fibers. Since the pressure vessels are straight pipes, they are not a receptacle and do not invalidate the water. After desalination, the water is stored in pools or in large tanks in order to put it through supplementary treatment processes. In the large desalination plants (such as those in the State of Israel), these reservoirs are built of concrete in the ground, and they do not invalidate the water. However, in small desalination plants there are sometimes portable tanks in which the water is treated, and in them the water becomes drawn. It turns out, then, that desalinated seawater in the State of Israel is fit for a mikva, and the question is whether it becomes invalid while it passes through the pipes.<br>That is, the problem is not in the sources of the water but in its passage through the pipes. Let us summarize the main doubts: <b>a)</b> Mishna Mikva'ot 4:3: \"One who hollows out a pipe to catch pebbles: in one of wood – any amount; in one of earthenware – a revi'it… A conduit (pipe) that is narrow at both ends and wide in the middle does not invalidate, because it was not made for receiving.\" And so too in SA 201:36. Thus, if the pipe has a depression intended to receive dirt, so that it will settle in it and not clog the pipe, the pipe is considered a vessel, and the water that passed through it is drawn. But if the place where the pipe widens was not intended to receive anything, but rather to increase the flow, it is not a vessel, and the water that flowed through it is not drawn. <b>b)</b> Bends in the pipe, since they are not intended to hold the water in them, are not considered a receptacle and do not make the pipe a vessel (Rambam, Hilkhot Mikva'ot 8:7; SA 201:36; and the overwhelming majority of the Aḥaronim). However, according to the Ra'avad, if the bend creates a receptacle, even if it was not intended for that, it makes the water drawn. Some were stringent like him (Beit Hillel 201:1; Leḥem 140; and others). Some hold that the Ra'avad was stringent in this only in hashaka for creating a mikva (Ḥiddushei Rabbi Ḥayim, Mikva'ot 6:4). In practice, the halakha follows the lenient opinion, but lekhatḥila one takes the opinion of the Ra'avad into account (Maharik 56; Da'at Kohen 113-114). <b>c)</b> In many places water meters are installed in the pipes, and for this purpose a widening is made in the pipe, which is considered a receptacle that invalidates the water (Da'at Kohen 128; Mikveh Yehuda; Minḥat Yitzḥak). Some permitted this, since the purpose of the widening in the pipe is not for the water to stay there, but on the contrary, for it to flow so that the meter can measure the water (Taharat Mayim 40). <b>d)</b> Some are stringent and hold that since at the end of the pipe there is a valve that stops the water, the pipe is considered a receptacle (Divrei Malkiel 2:64:4). Some hold that only if there is a faucet at the end of the pipe and the faucet itself has a receptacle is the water there considered drawn (Da'at Kohen 127). And some hold that since the pipe is not intended for storing the water but for conveying it, it is not considered a receptacle (Ḥatam Sofer, YD 218; Ḥazon Ish, YD 126:2). <b>e)</b> When the water is conveyed by means of pumps based on a pipe containing a piston for drawing out the air in the pipe and creating a vacuum that draws up the water, some say that since the pump was made in advance in such a way that it can raise the water, it has the law of a receptacle, which makes the water drawn and invalid (Sho'el U-meshiv, Mahadura 3, 2:122; Maharam Schick, YD 195). Others say that since the purpose of the pump is not to hold the water within it, and in addition it is not able to hold the water within it without an external force, it is not considered a receptacle (Naḥal Eshkol 53:3; Maharsham 2:85; Taharat Mayim 40). <b>f)</b> When the water is conveyed by means of pumps that operate by centrifugal force, they must be submerged in water, and if a receptacle was prepared for this purpose, all the water passing through there becomes invalid.<br>Even if all these receptacles are attached to the ground, since they were made before being attached, even after being attached they are considered receptacles, as explained in Bava Batra 65b: \"The Rabbis taught: A pipe that one hollowed out and afterwards fixed in place invalidates the mikva; if one fixed it in place and afterwards hollowed it out, it does not invalidate the mikva\" (Rema 201:34). And according to the stringent ones, who hold that a mikva that is entirely drawn is invalid by Torah law, lekhatḥila one should be concerned even in a case where the receptacles were made after being attached to the ground (Noda Bi-Yehuda Tinyana, YD 142:8; see above, note 1). On the other hand, some say that if all the use of these vessels and pipes is while attached to the ground, they are considered like a vessel that was first fixed in place and afterwards hollowed out, which does not invalidate the water (Naḥal Eshkol 53:3; Yad David, Hilkhot Mikva'ot, section 1; Gidulei Tahara, Responsum 5; Taharat Mayim 40).<br>The lenient ones added a further line of reasoning: according to many, as long as the water is connected to the spring and continues to flow even after passing through the receptacle, it remains kosher. Likewise, if the source is a lake, then if the pipes have the measure of 'kishfoferet ha-nod', there is room to say that there is hashaka between the mikva and the water coming out of the pipes (Maharival 3:70; Knesset Ha-gedola, Hagahot Beit Yosef 53; Mikveh Yisrael (Deiches), p. 13). However, nowadays no place is known where there is continuous hashaka from the spring to the water in the house, and not all the pipes have the diameter of 'kishfoferet ha-nod' (about 4 cm, and lekhatḥila 5 cm).<br>A further discussion concerns 'formed by a person': some say that when the faucet is opened, there is a concern that the water becomes invalid because its formation is by the person who opened the faucet, and therefore it is proper to pour away the first water so that it does not enter the mikva (see Hagahot Ḥatam Sofer on SA 201:48). Others are lenient, since opening the faucet is merely the removal of an obstruction, and there is no invalidity in this. In any case, the entire concern is regarding the first water that comes out after the faucet is opened, for only regarding that water do some argue that its formation is by a person, and it does not contain the measure of three log (Ḥatam Sofer 214; Da'at Kohen 127). Likewise regarding pumps, some say that since a person operates them, the water is considered as formed by a person (Darkhei Teshuva 201:198, citing Levushei Mordekhai; Har Tzvi 177). In contrast, many hold that only with manual drawing does the water become invalid, but with a pump that operates by steam or electricity, the water does not become invalid (Naḥal Eshkol 53:3; Maharam Schick, YD 156; Maharsham 1:44).<br>The stringent ones were further concerned that if making a mikva from tap water were permitted, people would eventually err and make a mikva in vessels. They were further concerned that if people wished to make a mikva at home, they would err and make it in an ordinary bathtub, which does not contain forty se'a (310 liters; and some require much more, as explained in halakha 3). In addition, some say that since the bathtub was made as a detached vessel, even after it is attached it is considered a vessel, and one may not make a mikva in it without first making a hole in it in order to nullify its status as a vessel, because according to many the drain hole is not effective, since it is part of the vessel's construction (above, end of note 1); and even according to those who hold that the drain hole can be effective, some say that as long as its diameter is not 7.6 cm it is not effective (ibid.). Furthermore, the structure of the bathtub makes it very difficult to immerse the entire body at once. Furthermore, sometimes when one enters the bathtub some water flows out into the upper drain opening, and then some say that the mikva becomes invalid under the law of flowing water (as explained in note 6). There is also a concern that when the tevila takes place at home, there will be women immersing who will err and become accustomed to immersing improperly, without a balanit (mikva attendant) to point out their error. On the other hand, some wrote that if a kosher tevila can be arranged at home, women who for various reasons refrain from coming to the public mikva will be strengthened in the mitzva (Mikveh Yehuda)."}]},{"slug":"18-10-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/18-10-13/","title_he":"יג – התחדשות הטהרה בישראל","title_en":"The Renewal of Purity in Israel","body_en":"<p>As a result of the sin, tum'a clung to man, closing off his heart and impairing his ability to connect his thoughts, deeds and emotions to the source of his life. And since his connection to God was damaged, death clung to him. The system of mitzvot dealing with all types of tum'a and with purification from them, which is connected mainly to the Temple, is intended to raise man up from the tum'a that clung to his physical aspects and to connect him to the source of his life. Through tevila in the mikva, one who is tamei returns to the primary element of life, and thereby he can connect to God, the Source of life, ascend to the Temple, and draw life into the land and into the body, into the imagination and the emotions and all the human talents, so that they will be connected to God and will draw blessing and tikkun (repair) into the world. But for Torah study and the fulfillment of the mitzvot, which are connected to the spiritual side of man, there is no need to immerse; rather, the preparation required for them is faith and good character traits, without any need to prepare the body and the flesh for this. As the Sages said (Berakhot 22a): \"Words of Torah are not susceptible to tum'a, as it is stated (Yirmiyahu 23:29): 'Is not My word like fire, says the Lord' – just as fire is not susceptible to tum'a, so too words of Torah are not susceptible to tum'a\" (see Orot Ha-teḥiya 35, by our master Rav Kook).</p>\n<p>Since the Temple was destroyed and Israel was exiled from its land, purity ceased from Israel, and only one purity remained for us, akin to the holiness of the Temple: the tevila of a woman for her purity, in order to reveal the life and holiness in the body, in the love and joy between husband and wife, which is the pinnacle of the fulfillment of the mitzva \"You shall love your fellow as yourself,\" which is equal to all the mitzvot. For the main aim of tevila and purity is to purify actual, concrete life, with the body and the flesh, the imagination and the emotions, so that they can connect to the Source of life and grow in strength without limit.</p>\n<p>May it be His will that through our return to our land we will merit to grow in the Torah of the Land of Israel and in its guidance for the repair of the world, and that through the purity and joy in the union of man and woman, blessing will spread to all areas of life, and we will merit the building of the Third Temple. And from the Holy of Holies the purity of the faith of Israel will be drawn forth to the whole world, in order to purify all the beliefs that are revealed in physical life with all its components. As the Sages said (Yoma 77b): \"The spring that issues from the Holy of Holies at first resembles the antennae of locusts (very thin); when it reaches the entrance of the Sanctuary it becomes like a thread of the warp; when it reaches the Hall it becomes like a thread of the woof; when it reaches the entrance of the Courtyard it becomes like the mouth of a small flask.\" Its further flow is described in the prophecy of Yeḥezkel (47:1-12): \"…and behold, water was issuing from under the threshold of the House…\", and the waters of the stream will go on and increase: after they have gone a thousand ammot they will reach a person's ankles, and after another thousand ammot they will reach the knees, and after another thousand ammot they will reach the loins, and after another thousand ammot a person will no longer be able to cross them; and so they will continue to grow ever stronger: \"And behold, on the bank of the stream were very many trees, on this side and on that… and they shall come into the sea, into the sea of the issuing waters (the Salt Sea, which is the Sea of Death), and the waters shall be healed. And it shall come to pass that every living creature that swarms, wherever the streams come, shall live, and there shall be a very great multitude of fish, for these waters have come there and they shall be healed, and everything shall live wherever the stream comes… And by the stream, upon its bank, on this side and on that, shall grow every tree for food, whose leaf shall not wither nor shall its fruit fail; it shall bring forth new fruit every month, because its waters issue from the Sanctuary; and its fruit shall be for food, and its leaf for healing.\" That is, since we will merit to be purified from tum'a and to connect to God with the full forces of life, the place where the people of Sedom sinned with the lust for money and all their other physical lusts, until it was turned into a place of death, will return to life, and its fish will multiply, and around it wondrous trees will grow, bearing fruit all year round, for every month new fruits will grow, and their leaves will be leaves of healing.</p>\n<p>Likewise in the prophecy of Yoel (4:18): \"And it shall come to pass on that day that the mountains shall drip with sweet wine, and the hills shall flow with milk, and all the streams of Yehuda shall flow with water, and a spring shall issue from the House of the Lord and shall water the Valley of Shittim.\" The Valley of Shittim alludes to the sin of the lust for harlotry, as it is stated (Bamidbar 25:1): \"And Israel dwelt in Shittim, and the people began to commit harlotry with the daughters of Moav.\" And the spring that will issue from the House of the Lord will purify and repair this lust as well, which will be transformed into a longing of love between a man and his wife, and for all things of holiness, and thereby blessing will increase throughout the land.</p>\n<p>However, the spring issuing from the Temple will purify and heal not only the Salt Sea; rather, as is alluded to in the prophecy of Yeḥezkel and stated explicitly in the prophecy of Zekharya, half of it will turn to the Mediterranean Sea, and thereby hashaka will be created between the spring issuing from the Temple, by way of the Mediterranean Sea, and the ocean that surrounds all the continents, so that through their contact with the spring issuing from the Temple, all the waters in the world will be purified and sanctified. As it is stated (Zekharya 14:8-9): \"And it shall come to pass on that day that living waters shall go out from Jerusalem, half of them toward the eastern sea (the Salt Sea) and half of them toward the western sea (the Mediterranean Sea); in summer and in winter it shall be. And the Lord shall be King over all the earth; on that day the Lord shall be One and His name One.\"</p>\n<p>And the tum'a of nidda will be abolished, as the Sages said: \"There is no prohibition greater than that of nidda, for a woman sees blood and the Holy One, blessed be He, forbade her to her husband, but in the future to come He will permit her, as it is stated (Zekharya 13:2): …'and I will cause the spirit of impurity to pass from the land'\" (Shoḥer Tov, Tehillim 146). And even the tum'a of death will pass from the world, as the Sages said: \"The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: In this world you would become pure and then become impure again, but in the future to come I will purify you with an eternal purity, so that you will not become impure, as it is stated (Yeḥezkel 36:25): 'And I will sprinkle pure water upon you, and you shall be pure; from all your impurities and from all your idols I will purify you'\" (Tanḥuma, Metzora 9). And death will be abolished from the world, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 11:6-9): \"And the wolf shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid… and the lion shall eat straw like the ox. And the nursing child shall play over the hole of the asp, and the weaned child shall put his hand on the adder's den. They shall not hurt nor destroy in all My holy mountain, for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the Lord, as the waters cover the sea.\" And it is stated (Yeshayahu 25:8): \"He will swallow up death forever, and the Lord God will wipe away tears from all faces, and the reproach of His people He will remove from all the earth, for the Lord has spoken.\"</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
